<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The floating dictation card — RADICALLY MINIMAL.

  ## What is present at rest, and what is not

  At rest this card contains the transcript, one mic control, and the four
  actions. Removed by the 2026-10 design pass:

    * the "Dicta" title TextView — the card only ever appears because the user
      just tapped a Dicta trigger, so it named the app to someone who had just
      named it themselves;
    * the full-bleed mic Button, which is now a centred pill.

  Kept, because the layout is not the only consumer: @id/status_text,
  @id/dictated_text, @id/mic_button, @id/attachment_note, @id/copy_button,
  @id/screenshot_button, @id/share_button, @id/close_button and
  @id/dictate_card all still exist with the same ids and the same view types.

  ## 🔴 THE OVERFLOW IS GONE — do not bring it back

  The design pass hid the four actions behind an @id/more_button overflow, with
  a TextWatcher auto-revealing @id/action_row once the transcript went
  non-empty, and @string/dictate_hint naming the "⋯" route in words. All three
  mechanisms existed to make a HIDDEN control findable.

  The operator's instruction retires the problem rather than the mitigations:
  "remove the button to toggle action buttons. Just always show the action
  buttons." So @id/more_button, its two strings, its drawable and its ghost
  background are deleted, @id/action_row carries no visibility attribute, and
  nothing in DictateActivity sets one. OverlayMinimalLayoutTest pins the row
  visible in the XML default AND in every transcript state, and its icon-control
  ledger fails if a fifth ImageButton reappears.

  ## Why still a ScrollView

  Unchanged reason: the window is a floating dialog with WRAP_CONTENT height,
  and on a landscape phone (~411dp usable, minus the 12dp window inset top and
  bottom) the column can be taller than the window. Without a scroll container
  the bottom of the card is clipped off rather than scrolled. Pinned by
  FloatingWindowSizeTest.theCardScrollsRatherThanClipping.
-->
<ScrollView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:background="@drawable/card_bg"
    android:clipToOutline="true">

    <!--
      🔴 focusable + focusableInTouchMode on the CARD ROOT is not decoration.

      @id/dictated_text is the only other view that can hold focus in touch
      mode, and DictateActivity.renderPreview refuses to overwrite a FOCUSED
      transcript box ("don't clobber the cursor while the user is editing") —
      so a box that holds focus silently swallows an incoming transcript.
      `parkFocusOffTheTranscript()` needs somewhere to put focus instead, and
      it used to use @id/mic_button. That control is now GONE in exactly the
      states the park is needed in (a hidden view cannot take focus), so the
      target has to be a view that is always present. This is it.
    -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/dictate_card"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:focusable="true"
        android:focusableInTouchMode="true"
        android:padding="20dp">

        <!--
          ========================= THE STATUS STRIP =========================
          🔴 ISSUE #9: @id/status_text USED TO LIVE IN THIS ROW and no longer
          does. It was maxLines=1 + ellipsize=end, sharing a 801px strip with
          the 72dp meter, which left it 555px — about 30 characters. The
          longest strings it carries (@string/stt_fell_back_retrying,
          @string/stt_tier_remote_only) are several times that, so the user
          never saw the half of the sentence that says what happens NEXT
          ("…the server transcript is on its way"). No amount of maxLines
          rescues it inside this row. It therefore sits BELOW the strip at
          full card width. Pinned by OverlayMinimalLayoutTest's
          theStatusLineSpansTheFullCardWidth and
          noStatusStringOutgrowsItsLineBudget.

          ⚠ RETRACTED FIGURES, NOT CORRECTED ONES. This comment used to say
          those two strings "are 154 and 145 characters", and the width note
          further down said "strings up to 154". Both are false and appear
          never to have been true: measured against the live resources the
          two lengths match 154/145 in NO unit, raw or with a realistic %1$s
          filler, and neither string has changed since 843e4f2.

          🔴 They are deliberately NOT replaced with today's numbers — not
          even as a correction. A length copied into a comment is a claim
          nothing re-checks, which is exactly how these two survived long
          enough for a maintainer to budget copy against them, and a
          "corrected" figure goes stale the same way on the next copy edit.
          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest derives the longest status string from the
          resources, asserts it against the line budget, and prints the real
          string and its real length in the failure message. That test is the
          only place a figure belongs.

          This row now carries two things, both GONE at rest. Both were
          RESERVED stubs
          for in-flight work on another branch; that branch (remote STT) has
          landed and both are now BOUND to real state. The reservation held —
          the merge was the mechanical swap the stubs were shaped for — so what
          follows documents the live contract, not a promise:

            @id/level_meter   a 4dp horizontal mic-level meter, 72dp x 4dp,
                              placed FIRST in the strip so it reads
                              left-to-right as "input is arriving" before the
                              words do. Now a ProgressBar (style
                              ?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal) with
                              @drawable/meter_progress — the reserved
                              @drawable/meter_track_bg track under a
                              @color/dicta_accent level. Bound by
                              DictateActivity.onLevel. Works with no partials:
                              deliberately independent of @id/dictated_text.

            @id/engine_badge  an 11sp pill (@drawable/badge_bg,
                              @color/dicta_on_accent_container at 9.84:1)
                              naming which engine produced the current text.
                              Bound by DictateActivity.showEngine(fromRemote)
                              to @string/engine_badge_remote or
                              @string/engine_badge_local — SHORT words, not the
                              status line's sentence. It used to carry
                              @string/stt_remote_done, which the status line
                              also carries for a plain remote success, so the
                              card said "Transcribed by the server" twice.
                              Still placed LAST, now
                              simply so the meter leads; the older reason ("so
                              it does not shift the status text") retired with
                              the #9 move — there is no longer any status text
                              in this row to shift. It carries a
                              contentDescription of its own because a short
                              badge is not self-describing to a screen reader.
                              (Reserved as @id/engine_badge, built on the
                              branch as @id/engine_label; this id won the
                              merge. See the view's own comment.)

          🔴 Do not encode engine or in-flight state in colour alone: the badge
          exists so there is always a WORD.

          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest pins this strip: the membership and ORDER of
          the three children, that the meter and the badge are GONE in the
          layout's at-rest default, that both are labelled, and — since the
          reservation was filled — that both actually become visible and
          carry text/level once a dictation drives them.
          =================================================================
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/overlay_status_strip"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical"
            android:baselineAligned="false">

            <!--
              The microphone level meter — the design's reserved 72dp x 4dp slot,
              now carrying the remote-STT branch's real implementation. The swap
              is the one this file's header prescribed: a ProgressBar styled
              ?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal, whose progressDrawable
              (@drawable/meter_progress) layers the reserved
              @drawable/meter_track_bg track under a @color/dicta_accent level.

              🔴 Not decoration. In the remote-only degradation tiers no partial
              text ever appears while recording, so this bar is the ONLY
              on-screen evidence that the microphone is live. Without it
              "nothing is moving" is indistinguishable from a dead capture — and
              a dead capture is a real state here, because Android enforces its
              one-capture-at-a-time policy by silently feeding the loser exact
              zeros. DictateActivity.onLevel binds progress; onClientSilenced
              flips it to INVISIBLE (not GONE) so the strip does not reflow when
              another app steals the mic.

              GONE is the LAYOUT default only — it is the at-rest state, before
              a dictation starts, not an "unbound stub" any more.
            -->
            <ProgressBar
                android:id="@+id/level_meter"
                style="?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal"
                android:layout_width="72dp"
                android:layout_height="4dp"
                android:layout_marginEnd="10dp"
                android:max="100"
                android:progress="0"
                android:progressDrawable="@drawable/meter_progress"
                android:contentDescription="@string/level_meter_desc"
                android:visibility="gone" />

            <!-- Pushes the badge to the trailing edge now that the status
                 text no longer takes the row's spare weight. -->
            <Space
                android:id="@+id/status_strip_spacer"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="1dp"
                android:layout_weight="1" />

            <!--
              Which engine produced the text currently in the box. The server
              and the phone disagree, sometimes materially, so the user has to
              be able to tell them apart rather than trust whatever appeared.
              DictateActivity.showEngine(fromRemote) binds the text.

              🔴 This view was @id/engine_label on the remote-STT branch and
              @id/engine_badge in the design pass's reservation. ONE id
              survived the merge — this one — because the design side owns more
              references (this layout, @string/engine_badge_desc,
              @drawable/badge_bg, OverlayMinimalLayoutTest's strip ledger) and
              because "badge" names what it renders as. The branch's two
              references were renamed to match.

              maxLines=1: the strip is one row, and the two bound strings are
              short. If longer copy ever lands here it must shrink, not reflow
              the card — the long-form fallback explanation belongs in
              @id/status_text, which already takes the weight and ellipsizes.
            -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/engine_badge"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:paddingStart="8dp"
                android:paddingEnd="8dp"
                android:paddingTop="2dp"
                android:paddingBottom="2dp"
                android:background="@drawable/badge_bg"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:ellipsize="end"
                android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_accent_container"
                android:contentDescription="@string/engine_badge_desc"
                android:visibility="gone" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!--
          The live status line — the card's first words.

          🔴 FULL CARD WIDTH, AND NOT ONE LINE. This is the #9 fix. It used to
          be a weighted child of @id/overlay_status_strip above, capped at
          maxLines=1 with ellipsize=end, which on this 1080px device left it
          555px — roughly 30 characters — for status copy several times that
          long (the figure "strings up to 154" that stood here is retracted;
          see the STATUS STRIP comment above for why no number replaces it).
          Everything after the first clause was replaced by an ellipsis,
          including the half that tells the user what happens next.

          maxLines=4 at full width clears the longest string with margin —
          the budget is REQUIRED_STATUS_LINES x CHARS_PER_STATUS_LINE in
          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest, which owns both constants, rather than an
          arithmetic result restated here. ellipsize=end is kept only as a
          backstop for copy added later; if it ever actually engages, this
          view needs another line, not a shorter sentence — the card is a
          ScrollView and can afford the height.

          🔴 WHAT IS AND IS NOT PINNED. The guard is a CHARACTER-COUNT budget:
          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest derives the longest status string from the
          resources and compares its LENGTH against the budget above, and
          separately asserts this view's real inflated width equals the card's
          inner width. It does NOT measure rendered text, and **rendered wrap
          is not pinned at all** — it cannot be under Robolectric, whose
          measureText returns a flat 1.0px per character and whose
          getEllipsisCount returns 0 even for a 4000-character string (that
          dead end is written up in the test). Whether the words actually fit
          on this device is confirmed by screenshot, not by the suite.

          ⚠ This paragraph used to end "OverlayMinimalLayoutTest measures the
          real longest string against the real inflated width rather than
          trusting this arithmetic." No test does that, and the file's own
          dead-end note says it is impossible by construction — so a
          maintainer could read it, believe wrap was covered, widen the copy,
          and ship a status line that truncates on device with every test
          green.
        -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/status_text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="2dp"
            android:text="@string/dictate_hint"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            android:maxLines="4"
            android:ellipsize="end"
            android:lineSpacingExtra="1dp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant" />

        <!--
          The transcript: the hero, and the reason the card exists. 17sp rather
          than the platform default because this is the content, not a form
          field, and minLines 3 rather than 4 — with the title and the action
          row gone the card no longer needs a tall box to look balanced.
          @color/dicta_on_surface measures 14.01:1 on the card.
        -->
        <EditText
            android:id="@+id/dictated_text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
            android:minLines="3"
            android:gravity="top"
            android:hint="@string/dictate_text_hint"
            android:textSize="17sp"
            android:lineSpacingExtra="3dp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface"
            android:textColorHint="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
            android:background="@drawable/input_bg"
            android:padding="14dp"
            android:inputType="textMultiLine|textCapSentences" />

        <!-- Shown only once a screenshot has actually been attached. The note is
             not decoration: many share targets drop EXTRA_TEXT when a stream is
             present, so the user needs to know Copy is the reliable text path. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/attachment_note"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
            android:visibility="gone"
            android:text="@string/screenshot_attached_note" />

        <!--
          The control row: the stop/fallback control, and (in the history mode
          only) the re-transcribe button. The overflow that used to share this
          row is gone — see the header.

          🔴 THE ROW IS EMPTY AT REST, AND THAT IS THE POINT. @id/mic_button is
          `gone` in this layout's default and stays gone unless DictateActivity
          puts it into one of its two remaining roles — STOP (a capture that is
          already running) or the mic-denied system-voice-typing fallback. The
          overlay used to show it labelled "Speak" on the finished transcript,
          which is a second recording one tap from the card that exists to show
          the first; the operator asked for that to go, so the role and
          @string/start_dictation went with it. Starting a recording is the
          floating bubble's job.

          @id/mic_button stays a Button (not an ImageButton) on purpose: the
          label is what keeps the recording state off colour-only footing. It
          is styled as an accent pill with the mic glyph as a compound drawable
          instead. The XML default label is "Stop" because that is the role any
          unlabelled reveal would be — never a start.

          OverlayNeverStartsARecordingTest pins both halves: this default, and
          that in every reachable card state the control is either GONE or
          carrying @string/stop_dictation / @string/use_system_voice_typing.
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
            android:gravity="center_vertical">

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/mic_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="56dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:background="@drawable/mic_pill_bg"
                android:drawableStart="@drawable/ic_mic"
                android:drawableTint="@color/dicta_on_accent"
                android:drawablePadding="8dp"
                android:paddingStart="22dp"
                android:paddingEnd="22dp"
                android:stateListAnimator="@null"
                android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_accent"
                android:textSize="15sp"
                android:textAllCaps="false"
                android:visibility="gone"
                android:text="@string/stop_dictation" />

            <!--
              Re-transcribe. GONE at rest and SHOWN ONLY in the history mode
              (DictateLaunch.EXTRA_HISTORY_ID), where @id/mic_button is hidden
              in the same breath.

              🔴 Why a separate control rather than relabelling the mic pill,
              which would have cost no XML at all: @id/mic_button's listener is
              `toggleDictation()`, i.e. it opens the microphone. A history card
              is the one state in this app where the microphone must not be
              touched — the whole point of RemoteTranscriber is an upload-only
              path — and "the same button, with a different label and a swapped
              listener" makes that a property of whichever assignment ran last.
              Two buttons makes it a property of the layout, and "mic_button is
              GONE on a history card" is then a state assertion a test can make.

              A Button, not an ImageButton: OverlayMinimalLayoutTest ledgers the
              ImageButton set precisely so an icon-only control cannot escape
              its label assertions, and this control is not icon-only — it is a
              word, which is what the repo's "never carry state by colour or
              position alone" rule wants here anyway.
            -->
            <Button
                android:id="@+id/retranscribe_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="56dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:background="@drawable/outline_button_bg"
                android:paddingStart="22dp"
                android:paddingEnd="22dp"
                android:stateListAnimator="@null"
                android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:textSize="15sp"
                android:textAllCaps="false"
                android:visibility="gone"
                android:text="@string/history_retranscribe" />

        </LinearLayout>

        <!--
          The actions. ALWAYS ON SCREEN — no default visibility attribute, and
          nothing in DictateActivity touches this row's visibility.

          🔴 It used to be GONE, revealed by an @id/more_button overflow or
          automatically once the transcript went non-empty. The operator asked
          for the toggle to go and the actions to be unconditional, so both
          reveal routes and the overflow itself were deleted. Do not
          re-introduce a "reveal" here: an action the user can see is the
          point, and the states where an action refuses ("Nothing to copy
          yet…") are handled by the action, not by hiding it.

          Still icon-only and still 4 children, unchanged ids and unchanged
          order. Each is 48dp tall (the minimum touch target) and each keeps
          BOTH contentDescription and tooltipText.

          🔴 Two ledgers cover this row: DictateActionViewTypesTest asserts its
          child-id set, and OverlayMinimalLayoutTest's icon-control ledger now
          holds exactly the same four — the overflow was the one icon control
          outside the row, and it is gone.
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/action_row"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:layout_marginTop="10dp">

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/copy_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_copy"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/copy"
                android:tooltipText="@string/copy" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/screenshot_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_screenshot"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/screenshot"
                android:tooltipText="@string/screenshot" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/share_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_send"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/share"
                android:tooltipText="@string/share" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/close_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_close"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/close"
                android:tooltipText="@string/close" />
        </LinearLayout>
    </LinearLayout>
</ScrollView>
